🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝗱𝗲 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝘁 𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗱𝘆 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “One Kubernetes node suddenly shows NotReady. How will you troubleshoot it in production?”

I wouldn't immediately reboot or remove the node.

First, I would understand why the node became NotReady and what workload impact it has.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check node status

kubectl get nodes

Then:

kubectl describe node <node-name>

I would check the Conditions and Events sections.

Look for:

🔹 MemoryPressure
🔹 DiskPressure
🔹 PIDPressure
🔹 Kubelet issues
🔹 Network problems

2️⃣ Check workloads on the node

kubectl get pods -A -o wide | grep <node-name>

I want to know which applications are running on the affected node and whether production workloads are impacted.

3️⃣ Check node resource usage

kubectl top node <node-name>

Check CPU and memory usage, if metrics are available.

4️⃣ Check kubelet

If I have access to the node:

systemctl status kubelet

Check recent kubelet logs:

journalctl -u kubelet --since "30 minutes ago"

5️⃣ Check disk and filesystem

df -h
df -i

Disk exhaustion can cause node health problems.

6️⃣ Check container runtime

Depending on the environment:

systemctl status containerd

I would also investigate recent infrastructure, networking, OS, or configuration changes.

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Node Status → Conditions → Workloads → Resources → Kubelet → Container Runtime → Root Cause

If required, I would cordon/drain the node according to the approved production procedure before performing maintenance.

kubectl cordon <node-name>

After the issue is fixed, I would verify:

kubectl get nodes

and confirm that affected workloads are healthy.

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

NotReady is a symptom. Don't restart the node blindly.

First understand what caused the node to become unhealthy and what impact it has on workloads. 🚀
